Anorexia, nausea, vomiting, and constipation may occur especially if high doses are given [3]. Large doses in elderly individuals may cause fecal impaction [3]. Rare instances of intestinal necrosis have been reported [3]. Intestinal obstruction due to concretions of aluminum hydroxide, when used in combination with sodium polystyrene sulfonate, has been reported [3][6]. 

The following events have been reported from worldwide post marketing experience: 
• Fecal impaction following rectal administration, particularly in children [3];
• Gastrointestinal concretions (bezoars) following oral administration [3];
• Ischemic colitis, gastrointestinal tract ulceration or necrosis which could lead to intestinal perforation [3];
• Rare cases of acute bronchitis and/or bronchopneumonia associated with inhalation of particles of polystyrene sulfonate [3].

Note: The sources do not specifically mention the risks of an overdose of sodium polystyrene sulfonate, but rather the potential side effects of high doses or long-term use.
